WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.829 --> 00:00:04.614
The line organisation, the departments that run the daily work,

2
00:00:04.823 --> 00:00:06.321
owns it after you leave.

3
00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:11.471
Name one process owner per new process, close to the daily work,

4
00:00:11.634 --> 00:00:14.002
like a planning manager; not a staff role.

5
00:00:15.024 --> 00:00:16.278
They own the KPI,

6
00:00:16.522 --> 00:00:20.771
the key performance indicator: the number that shows whether it works.

7
00:00:21.775 --> 00:00:25.687
Alone, the owner must know what is normal, chair, train new people,

8
00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:28.021
solve problems and know whom to call.

9
00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:33.067
Put it on a handover checklist and tick it off together, in real work,

10
00:00:33.252 --> 00:00:34.599
not in a presentation.

11
00:00:35.911 --> 00:00:40.833
Hand over in steps: the owner watches, runs it with you beside, then alone.

12
00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:43.650
Each step a few weekly meetings.

13
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:49.953
Put the KPIs in the line's own reports; a separate dashboard dies when you leave.

14
00:00:50.954 --> 00:00:51.546
Reluctant?

15
00:00:52.276 --> 00:00:54.702
Ask what they need to run it without you.

16
00:00:55.408 --> 00:00:57.312
If not, the sponsor must know.

17
00:00:58.368 --> 00:01:03.569
In IT, extra support after go-live is called hypercare: often two to six weeks,

18
00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:04.522
with an end date.

19
00:01:05.589 --> 00:01:09.258
Wrong: a final presentation and a folder nobody opens.

20
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:12.765
Ask: has the owner solved anything without me yet?

21
00:01:14.135 --> 00:01:19.232
A cable manufacturer: Sanne, the planning manager, takes over the weekly meeting.

22
00:01:19.977 --> 00:01:22.253
92 percent on time is normal.

23
00:01:23.244 --> 00:01:28.271
Her checklist: read the KPI, chair, explain the capacity model to a new planner,

24
00:01:28.445 --> 00:01:29.652
handle rush orders.

25
00:01:30.685 --> 00:01:33.518
Weeks 1 to 3 you chair; 4 to 6 she does,

26
00:01:33.623 --> 00:01:37.385
with you beside and a 10 minute debrief; 7 to 9 alone.

27
00:01:38.423 --> 00:01:43.195
The proof: in week 8 a machine breaks down and she re-plans without calling.

28
00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:46.295
The KPI joins the monthly report.

29
00:01:47.653 --> 00:01:48.408
When is it done?

30
00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:53.706
The acceptance criteria say so: measurable, and agreed at the start.

31
00:01:54.809 --> 00:01:59.824
They go in the project charter: the agreement on goal, scope, roles and timing.

32
00:02:00.839 --> 00:02:05.124
For example: cancellations below 8 percent, six weeks running.

33
00:02:05.821 --> 00:02:09.676
Write down who signs; otherwise it ends when someone tires.

34
00:02:10.662 --> 00:02:15.666
The sign-off is the sponsor's yes: each criterion with evidence on one page,

35
00:02:15.805 --> 00:02:17.291
met, partly or not.

36
00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:21.934
That protects both sides: you can close and invoice.

37
00:02:22.658 --> 00:02:25.920
Otherwise the last 10 percent drags on for months.

38
00:02:27.242 --> 00:02:31.154
Not everything is perfect: put what remains on an open points list,

39
00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.952
with item, owner and date, and accept.

40
00:02:34.998 --> 00:02:36.530
That owner sits in the line,

41
00:02:36.717 --> 00:02:41.431
the normal hierarchy; points left with the temporary team die when the project ends.

42
00:02:42.568 --> 00:02:47.282
Experts argue: accept on outputs, like a trained team, or on outcomes,

43
00:02:47.479 --> 00:02:48.826
which often take months?

44
00:02:49.848 --> 00:02:53.993
PRINCE2 closes on outputs and plans a benefits review later.

45
00:02:54.793 --> 00:02:59.925
A middle: outputs, weeks of outcomes, and a review after three to six months.

46
00:03:00.956 --> 00:03:01.745
New wishes?

47
00:03:02.458 --> 00:03:05.720
Check against the criteria; the wishes go to the line.

48
00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:09.324
Ask at the kick-off: what would you need to see to sign?

49
00:03:10.599 --> 00:03:14.175
The charter: at least 90 percent on time for eight weeks;

50
00:03:14.337 --> 00:03:17.066
the planning manager chairs; the model explained.

51
00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:22.437
On one page: 91 to 94 percent for nine weeks: met.

52
00:03:23.152 --> 00:03:25.045
Chairing since week 7: met.

53
00:03:26.102 --> 00:03:29.910
The model is not yet explained to the second planner: partly met.

54
00:03:30.947 --> 00:03:34.454
The sponsor wants a sales forecast link, never in scope.

55
00:03:35.203 --> 00:03:37.943
You: good idea, an open point for IT.

56
00:03:38.909 --> 00:03:43.379
Open: train the second planner by 15 May; assess the link by June.

57
00:03:44.107 --> 00:03:45.303
The sponsor signs.

58
00:03:46.648 --> 00:03:49.876
Expect the three-month dip: results often slide.

59
00:03:50.651 --> 00:03:52.254
A rule of thumb, not a law.

60
00:03:53.309 --> 00:03:57.198
Attention moves on, people change jobs, a busy season comes,

61
00:03:57.396 --> 00:03:59.114
exceptions become habits.

62
00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:00.734
Plan for it.

63
00:04:01.735 --> 00:04:05.717
Short checks keep it alive: every week the line manager checks the board,

64
00:04:05.822 --> 00:04:07.622
the checklist and the actions.

65
00:04:08.649 --> 00:04:11.807
Car makers made it the layered process audit: daily,

66
00:04:11.982 --> 00:04:14.211
weekly, monthly, layer by layer.

67
00:04:14.926 --> 00:04:17.480
Small and often beats one big audit.

68
00:04:18.531 --> 00:04:22.908
A monthly KPI review with the team: trend, reasons, actions.

69
00:04:23.625 --> 00:04:26.156
Watch the trend; one bad week is noise.

70
00:04:27.502 --> 00:04:32.505
Agree a follow-up in advance, a planned visit at one, three and six months.

71
00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.672
And a trigger: three weeks below 85 percent on time, and the owner calls.

72
00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.729
So coming back is a plan, not a failure.

73
00:04:42.726 --> 00:04:43.144
Slipping?

74
00:04:43.828 --> 00:04:47.520
Do not fix it yourself; help the owner find what changed.

75
00:04:48.226 --> 00:04:49.434
Some slipping is normal.

76
00:04:50.433 --> 00:04:52.221
Does it hang on one enthusiast?

77
00:04:52.996 --> 00:04:55.655
Check early that a second person can run it.

78
00:04:56.703 --> 00:04:57.794
How long do you stay?

79
00:04:58.485 --> 00:04:59.379
Experts differ.

80
00:05:00.117 --> 00:05:03.612
Test: what have you changed in the process since I left, without me?

81
00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:08.465
A food plant: from 72 to 90 percent on time.

82
00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:12.205
After three months 81: half the gain is gone.

83
00:05:13.229 --> 00:05:17.002
With the owner you look at what changed: two new planners never trained,

84
00:05:17.211 --> 00:05:19.545
the weekly review cancelled four times.

85
00:05:20.574 --> 00:05:23.209
He decides: the senior planner trains them,

86
00:05:23.418 --> 00:05:26.786
and the weekly review is fixed in the calendar, with a stand-in.

87
00:05:27.782 --> 00:05:30.696
Six weeks later you look again: 88 percent.
